<ui:FluentWindow x:Class="ChronoMock.App.Views.MessageDialog"
                 xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                 xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
                 xmlns:ui="http://schemas.lepo.co/wpfui/2022/xaml"
                 xmlns:d="http://schemas.microsoft.com/expression/blend/2008"
                 xmlns:mc="http://schemas.openxmlformats.org/markup-compatibility/2006"
                 mc:Ignorable="d"
                 Width="440"
                 SizeToContent="Height"
                 MinHeight="0"
                 ResizeMode="NoResize"
                 ShowInTaskbar="False"
                 WindowStartupLocation="CenterOwner"
                 ExtendsContentIntoTitleBar="True"
                 WindowBackdropType="None">
  <!--
    The app's own dialog, for the questions and notices of NORMAL operation (zasady/13). The native
    MessageBox it replaces had two faults, and only one of them was that it is a light box on a dark app:
    its buttons are labelled by WINDOWS, in the system language, so an English interface answered in
    Polish, and no translation key of ours was involved at all (rule 15).

    The three last-resort dialogs in App.xaml.cs deliberately stay native. A themed window needs the
    theme dictionaries and the strings, which are exactly what those three report as broken.

    Layout follows the shape a dialog has to have to be read in one glance: the question as a heading
    inside the content rather than as title-bar chrome (a FluentWindow's title is small, grey and easy to
    miss), the detail under it, and the answers on a footer of their own so the eye finds them without
    hunting. The destructive answer carries the failure red of the session Stop control, so "this ends
    something" looks the same wherever it appears - with the wording carrying the meaning either way,
    never the colour alone (zasady/13 9).

    The background sits on the content root below, for the reason written out in MainWindow.xaml: a
    Background on a ui:FluentWindow paints nothing at all. This dialog carried the same dead attribute,
    and it showed - measured on the open dialog, 63.68 percent of its surface was the wpfui default
    rather than our base, while the footer it sits beside was ours all along.
  -->
  <Grid Background="{DynamicResource BrushBgBase}">
    <!-- The message row takes the star so the footer stays against the bottom edge whatever height the
         frame settles on, rather than leaving an unexplained empty strip under the buttons. The height
         itself is settled by the MinHeight of 0 above, and that number was measured rather than guessed:
         a FluentWindow carries a minimum height of its own, so sizing to content stopped at 320 px and
         left about 60 of them empty. Removing the extended title bar changed nothing, which is what ruled
         the frame out; adding 300 px to the message grew the window by only 200, which is what showed the
         floor. Nothing here can be shrunk below its content, because the window cannot be resized and
         always sizes to what it holds. -->
    <Grid.RowDefinitions>
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
      <RowDefinition Height="*" />
      <RowDefinition Height="Auto" />
    </Grid.RowDefinitions>

    <!-- Chrome only: no maximize or minimize on a dialog, and the close button means the same as the
         cancelling answer below it. -->
    <ui:TitleBar Grid.Row="0" ShowMaximize="False" ShowMinimize="False"
                 Title="{Binding Title, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType={x:Type ui:FluentWindow}}}" />

    <StackPanel Grid.Row="1" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXl}" VerticalAlignment="Top">
      <TextBlock x:Name="Heading" TextWrapping="Wrap"
                 FontSize="{StaticResource FontSizeSubtitle}" FontWeight="SemiBold"
                 Foreground="{DynamicResource BrushTextPrimary}" />
      <TextBlock x:Name="Message" TextWrapping="Wrap" Margin="{StaticResource SpaceSm}"
                 FontSize="{StaticResource FontSizeBody}"
                 Foreground="{DynamicResource BrushTextSecondary}" />
    </StackPanel>

    <Border Grid.Row="2" Background="{DynamicResource BrushBgSurface}" Padding="{StaticResource SpaceLg}">
      <StackPanel Orientation="Horizontal" HorizontalAlignment="Right">
        <!-- The affirmative sits first, as it does in every Windows dialog this one replaces. Keyboard
             focus starts on the cancelling answer instead, because the affirmative is the one that
             cannot be taken back. -->
        <!-- The failure red rather than the accent, and the same red the session Stop control wears, so
             "this ends something" looks the same wherever it appears. A question only reaches this window
             when it guards something that cannot be taken back, so there is no benign affirmative to tell
             apart yet - the day one arrives it needs a fill of its own, not this one by default. -->
        <Button x:Name="AffirmativeButton" Click="OnAffirmativeClick"
                Style="{StaticResource PrimaryActionButton}"
                Background="{DynamicResource BrushStatusFails}"
                MinWidth="{StaticResource DialogButtonMinWidth}"
                Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}" />
        <!-- Its label is set in code-behind, because it says Cancel next to a question and Close on a
             notice, and the two shapes are one window. -->
        <Button x:Name="CancelButton" Click="OnCancelClick" IsCancel="True"
                MinWidth="{StaticResource DialogButtonMinWidth}"
                Padding="{StaticResource SpaceMd}"
                Margin="{StaticResource SpaceXs}" />
      </StackPanel>
    </Border>
  </Grid>
</ui:FluentWindow>
